\newlabel{fig:copulas}{{1}{8}{How a different pairing reverses mean motion in the concrete example of Section~\ref {sec:matrix-experiment}. Both problems have the same separate curvature and noise distributions and the same gradient covariance. \textbf {a:} For displacement $u=(x,-x)$, bars give the mean coordinate gradient per unit coordinate displacement, conditioned on that coordinate having zero noise (quiet) or unit noise (noisy). Equivalently, they show $\E [(Au)_i/u_i\mid |e_i|]$. A negative bar gives an outward descent component; it is not a negative Hessian eigenvalue. Ordinary averaging gives $.75$ in both cases. \textbf {b:} AdamW's local curvature weights are the same in both problems, but the quiet coordinate receives much more weight. These weights average over the full noise history; they are not instantaneous inverse-noise factors. Caps show deterministic certified bounds, not confidence intervals. \textbf {c:} After including weight decay, the local mean motion is $\dot x=-kx$. The weighted quiet contribution now makes the coupled case repel along this direction. Arrows show direction only in this local linearization, not simulated trajectories. Plotting code was developed with OpenAI Codex; displayed quantities are derived from certified calculations}{figure.1}{}}
